        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="vae2-37" value=": Differentiation of &lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=15;" vertex="1" parent="vae2-figure">
          <mxGeometry x="409.5" y="349.3" width="161" height="21" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="vae2-38" value="Illustration of the reparameterization trick. The variational parameters &lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt; affect the objective &lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt; through the random variable &lt;b&gt;z&lt;/b&gt; ~ &lt;i&gt;q&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/sub&gt;(&lt;b&gt;z&lt;/b&gt;|&lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;). We wish to compute gradients ∇&lt;sub&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt; to optimize the objective with SGD. In the original form (left), we cannot differentiate &lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt; w.r.t. &lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, because we cannot directly backpropagate gradients through the random variable &lt;b&gt;z&lt;/b&gt;. We can 'externalize' the randomness in &lt;b&gt;z&lt;/b&gt; by re-parameterizing the variable as a deterministic and differentiable function of &lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;, and a newly introduced random variable &lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ε&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. This allows us to 'backprop through &lt;b&gt;z&lt;/b&gt;', and compute gradients ∇&lt;sub&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;ϕ&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt;." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=14.9;fontColor=#000000;" vertex="1" parent="vae2-figure">
          <mxGeometry x="0" y="420" width="700" height="115.3" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
